WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
To understand how React Native's new architecture achieves zero-copy execution,

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.500
we have to look at the mechanical choke point it replaced, the asynchronous message queue.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.300
This queue forced high-speed data to aggressively bottleneck.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.700
In the legacy architecture, the JavaScript engine, usually JavaScript core,

5
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.900
and the native layer operated in total isolation.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.500
They were completely siloed environments, connected only by this single message queue.

7
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.500
Without direct memory access, data was transformed for transport.

8
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.500
JS numbers, strings, and arrays were completely flattened into a JSON string to cross the gap,

9
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.500
forcing the native side to execute a full JSON parse to reconstruct the object in memory.

10
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:48.500
Continuous stringification between execution environments throttles the communication bandwidth of any high-frequency application.

11
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.500
This process incurs three specific penalties.

12
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:58.000
The first is CPU overhead, dedicated entirely to running the serialization and deserialization algorithms.

13
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:08.500
In absolute terms, this parse and string the si pipeline extracts a penalty of 0.1 to 5 milliseconds of execution time for every large function call.

14
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.500
This illustrates the second penalty, severe memory bloat.

15
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:19.000
As JavaScript allocates payload memory, serialization forces an exact duplicate into the native heap.

16
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
This guarantees a two times memory allocation for every cross-boundary message, holding both copies simultaneously until garbage collection runs.

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
The third penalty is a strict architectural constraint. All cross-boundary communication must be asynchronous.

18
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.500
Because of the queue, no call can block and wait for a response.

19
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:46.000
This breaks down when interacting with native APIs that strictly requires synchronous execution to function correctly,

20
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.500
like scroll event listeners or 60 frame per second layout measurements.

21
00:01:50.500 --> 00:02:02.500
Forcing synchronous native requirements through an inherently asynchronous pipeline required engineers to build complex workarounds, like interaction manager, just to maintain basic UI stability.

22
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:08.000
React Native 0.76 introduces the JavaScript interface, or JSI.

23
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
This architecture is designed explicitly to bypass the JSON queue entirely.

24
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
JSI is not a framework. It is a thin, pure C++ header-only library.

25
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.000
It wraps the JavaScript engine in a C++ interface, which eliminates the central queue barrier.

26
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.000
This interface is completely engine agnostic. It's not coupled exclusively to Hermes, nor to JavaScript core, or even to V8.

27
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Through the JSI.Runtime interface, swapping the underlying JavaScript engine requires zero changes to the C++ code interacting with it above that layer.

28
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Removing the central message queue redefines the communication layer, replacing asynchronous message passing with direct synchronized memory access.

29
00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Direct memory access introduces a central risk, allowing C++ to read JavaScript data can trigger memory leaks, or violate the JavaScript garbage collector.

30
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:10.000
To handle this securely, the architecture uses JSI.value as the core abstraction for holding JavaScript values within C++ space.

31
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:19.000
JSI.value operates as a tagged union. It points directly to any JavaScript data type in memory, without ever copying the underlying data.

32
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.000
To maintain safety, copying the subtraction is intentionally disabled by design.

33
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Copying the reference without the garbage collector tracking it creates a dangling pointer, the moment the original memory is freed.

34
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Instead, JSI enforces move only semantics. Ownership of the exact memory reference cleanly transfers to a new C++ variable, strictly maintaining one active reference line.

35
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:49.000
This explicit ownership design actively prevents the memory duplication tax that plagued the legacy architecture.

36
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Moving past data structures, we need to look at how executable functions and custom native modules are actually constructed in this architecture.

37
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:05.000
JSI.host object is the specific C++ class structure used to expose native hardware directly to JavaScript.

38
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:12.000
When JavaScript code reads a property on this object, it triggers a native get method through an unbroken execution path.

39
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:19.000
The inverse is also true, writing data to a JavaScript property immediately triggers the native C++ set method.

40
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:28.000
Directly exposing native classes via host object entirely eliminates the need to map out complex callback architectures when accessing deep OS level APIs.

41
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Let's look at a real-world edge case, reading immediate data from a native hardware sensor using pure JSI without any scaffolding or code generation.

42
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:45.000
On the JavaScript consumption side, the code simply assigns a value from the sensor.lastreading property. Notice the absence of asynchronous operators in this execution.

43
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
There is no the weight, no dot then, and no promise chain.

44
00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Down at the C++ layer, the hardware sensor class is defined natively as a JSI.host object and its get method specifically checks for the string last reading.

45
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:13.000
The moment the JavaScript engine evaluates sensor dot last reading, the JavaScript thread halts, it then immediately and synchronously invokes the C++ get method to physically pull the hardware sensor.

46
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:21.000
The C++ method evaluates the physical sensor and returns the precise double value directly back to the JavaScript thread in real time.

47
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:33.000
By replacing JSON cues with shared memory pointers and synchronous C++ execution, react native achieves the zero latency interoperability required for complex 60 frame per second native applications.

